    {
      "id": "v6/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top1/r00/bootstrap",
      "evidence_sha256": "41b687d93a7d194761a7ae90f76ab966144ef954a0eb18d0669204de99a4245d",
      "assessment": "Top1 bootstrap correctly starts with empty task knowledge, the frozen R0 and four one-factor probes [7,5,5,5], [5,3,5,5], [5,5,7,5], [5,5,5,3]. This covers all four control windows immediately, unlike a strategy that repeatedly tunes only the first window. It distinguishes coordinate control from indirect chemistry/affinity, retains competing late-overwrite and mapping explanations, and labels initial beliefs unresolved. However, its diagnosis calls the individual minimum RMSD 0.353 A the pool anchor for the 0.02 A band. The ranking unit is the mean of a 25-candidate shared schedule, initially 0.794651 A, not the best individual. Predictions mainly enumerate both possible signs rather than stating which observation would favor each explanation.",
      "decision_update": "All four proposals were executed in R1. Their condition deltas against R0 were +0.000643172, +0.000045967, -0.000146372 and +0.000420280 A, while all four affinity gaps narrowed slightly. These are small but signed observations to preserve; they do not establish a general monotone response across different controls. A useful capability hypothesis is to keep the early four-window coverage while requiring separate group-level ranking, candidate-level diagnostics and control-to-terminal sensitivity records. An unchanged clipping flag must not itself falsify amplitude transmission when delivered displacement changed.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "No prior search answer was imported, and the four-probe coverage is a positive counterexample to the old over-conservative exploration pattern. Bootstrap cannot know the response sign, so uncertainty itself is not an error. The failure is using the wrong statistical unit for the band and not specifying discriminating evidence. These findings do not justify a teacher-specific amplitude table or an early range expansion.",
