    {
      "id": "v6/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top1/r01/G2",
      "evidence_sha256": "0b68fb8d0e3fd0aeeb3609ca3e68d9786cbcfacd8a480829f2327bc420aea342",
      "assessment": "R1 G2 correctly scopes the small response of [5,3,5,5] versus R0 to control 1 on the all-5 background: condition +0.000045967 A, affinity-gap -0.000024052, all 25 valid. It proposes [3,3,5,5] and [5,3,5,7] to investigate an early down-direction and a late up-direction, rather than repeating the same tested array. It does not query displacement diagnostics and therefore appropriately leaves clipping/weak transmission/overwrite unresolved. But it anchors the ranking band to candidate c006 at 0.353777 A and describes a sparse candidate band, confusing group selection with individual reconstruction. It also treats a tiny measured effect as equivalence without independent evidence of an equivalence scale.",
      "decision_update": "Both proposals were selected. R2 [3,3,5,5] reaches condition 0.794144941 A with Mean 4.209961433, while [5,3,5,7] reaches 0.794291785 A with Mean 4.210039511. These results provide useful opposite-direction and condition/function-conflict evidence, despite small magnitudes. Preserve this budget shift toward unvisited controls. Improve the knowledge update by separating 'effect small on this edge' from 'axis ineffective', and by retaining raw signed observations alongside the ranking-band decision.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "Its unresolved mechanistic diagnosis is more defensible than G1's false no-op claim. It has no current sibling data by design, so proposing a direction studied by another private group is not itself redundant misconduct; the synthesizer must combine that information. It has not identified the high-condition-error candidates as actual intervention responders, and the evidence does not establish that affinity can change only when mean geometric error changes.",
